```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_1
diagnosis_text:
The OSPF adjacency between router_core_1 and router_core_3 has dropped because
the physical link on router_core_1's eth1 interface is down. The kernel reports
eth1 as state DOWN with no LOWER_UP or RUNNING flags, meaning the NIC has no
active link signal. The IP address 172.16.0.2/31 is still assigned to the
interface, and the FRR OSPF configuration correctly covers this subnet under
Area 0.0.0.0 (network 172.16.0.2/31 area 0), so the fault is not a
misconfiguration. FRR detects the kernel link-down event and explicitly marks
eth1 as "OSPF not enabled on this interface," suppressing all hello
transmissions toward router_core_3 (172.16.0.3). Because no hellos arrive,
router_core_3's dead timer for neighbor 172.16.0.0 (router_core_1) expires and
the Full adjacency is torn down. As a result router_core_1 loses its direct
backbone path to router_core_3 and the data-centre segment; it currently
reaches the 172.16.0.2/31 subnet as an OSPF learned route via router_core_2
(172.16.0.1 on eth0), which explains why connectivity is still intact but
redundancy is gone. The root cause is a link-layer failure on router_core_1
eth1 — the interface connecting router_core_1 to router_core_3 is down.
Corrective fix: restore the link by running `ip link set eth1 up` on
router_core_1 (and investigate whether a cable/veth fault or an administrative
shutdown caused the link-down event). Once the interface comes up, FRR will
automatically re-enable OSPF on eth1 and re-establish the Full adjacency with
router_core_3.
=== END ===
```